I read the aaAzureProductFeatureStore repo (151 SQL files, 117 configs, 116 run scripts) and combined it with the stats from the earlier steps. Below are the practices your lakehouse already follows, then a plan for the governance gap.
Best practices already in place
1. A metadata-driven framework (your strongest design choice)
•	Every load is defined by YAML rather than code: two global configs (PFS and PFUS) plus about 110 per-table configs. Each config lists the ordered SQL steps, the delete mode (date / all), the primary keys and the post-load checks.
•	SQL files are parameterised with @ placeholders (@source_schema, @target_schema, @tbl_*, @run_from_day_idnt). The same code runs in DEV, TST and PRD by switching config, never by editing SQL.
•	One generic engine, build_fs(), executes every table. A new feature table needs a config, its SQL files and a run script, with no new orchestration code. There is a bootstrap template (template_feature_config.yaml) for this.
•	Runtime controls (max_days_increment, max_parallel_jobs) let you tune backfills and incremental loads per table without code changes.
•	Naming is consistent end to end (ftr_{stream}_{feature} for table, config, SQL and job), which makes the lineage machine-parseable. That is what made the Featureverse knowledge graph possible.
2. Storage
•	All tables are Delta on ADLS, giving ACID writes, time travel and schema enforcement at about 118 TB.
•	Large tables are partitioned by DAY_IDNT, and channel tables by DAY_IDNT, CHANNEL_CDE. This matches both how the data is loaded (delete-and-reload by date) and how it is read (date-range filters).
•	File sizing is healthy: for example, ftr_pfs_item_loc_day is 9.3 TB across about 71K files (about 130 MB each), so there is no small-file problem.
•	Data is layered: source → work tables (*_wrk1, *_wrk2) → feature tables → wide item-location-day tables → views (*_channel_comp_vw) → gold (aa_smkt_pfs_gold).
•	Loads are idempotent: each run deletes the date window and rewrites it, so reruns and backfills are safe.
3. Source ingestion with shallow clones
•	Shallow-cloning the biw source tables gives you stable, point-in-time copies without copying the data. You avoid duplicate storage, each run sees a consistent snapshot, and the upstream EDP tables are protected from your heavy reads.
•	Source freshness is checked before each load (externalDependenciesBefore in modelstore_dependencies.yaml), so nothing builds on stale upstream data.
4. Compute
•	Transformations run on SQL warehouses (2X-Large for production, X-Large for interactive/dev). Batch and ad hoc work are separated, and each environment has its own warehouse (DISC/INT/OPS).
•	Instance pools and cluster policies are created by code (infra_deployment_utils.R), so infrastructure is reproducible.
•	Production runs as a service principal, not a named user.
5. Orchestration via the Jobs API
•	Jobs are triggered with the Jobs REST API (/api/2.0/jobs/run-now) from R, and job names follow ade-aaazureproductfeaturestore-<table>.
•	Dependencies live in one place (modelstore_dependencies.yaml): hard internal parent dependencies plus external source checks.
•	Post-load checks (check_step: daily counts, primary-key uniqueness per date) act as built-in data quality gates.
6. Unity Catalog and sharing
•	Each environment has its own catalog (uc_0004_dev/tst/prd_prd_catalog) with the same schema names, so promotion is just a catalog switch.
•	Consumers get published tables (tables_to_publish), views and a gold schema, which keeps them away from the work tables.
