The governance gap: what's missing
•	No table or column comments, TBLPROPERTIES or tags in any DDL.
•	No grants as code, and no data classification.
•	Your user can't query system.access (audit and lineage). That is why I couldn't link queries to tables earlier.
Recommended governance plan, in four phases
Phase 1: Foundations
•	Define a governed-tag set at account level so tag values are controlled: 
o	domain: pricing, promo, sales, inventory, supply
o	sensitivity: public, internal, confidential, restricted
o	data_owner and tier: work, feature, gold
o	pii: true/false
o	refresh_sla
•	Add comments to tables and columns. Your Featureverse column lineage and the YAML configs can generate most of them; Unity Catalog's AI-generated comments can fill the rest.
•	Request access to system.access (audit and lineage) and system.information_schema tag views for monitoring.
Phase 2: Automated classification
•	Turn on Unity Catalog data classification for the aa_smkt_pfs_v3 and gold schemas. It scans columns, detects sensitive classes (names, emails, phone numbers, addresses, card numbers and so on) and applies system classification tags automatically. New tables and columns are picked up as they appear.
•	Add a metadata-driven tagging step to your framework, so it stays consistent with your config-first design: 
o	Each feature config gets a governance block (domain, owner, sensitivity, column overrides).
o	A post-load step in build_fs() applies those tags and comments after each run.
o	New tables are tagged from day one, and the YAML stays the single source of truth.
•	Most of your data is product, location, price and sales features with little PII. The customer and customer_group features and the Coles Online tables are the likely sensitive candidates, so review them first.
Phase 3: Policy enforcement
•	Use attribute-based access control (ABAC) policies tied to governed tags rather than per-table grants: 
o	Column masks: any column tagged pii or sensitivity=restricted is masked automatically unless the user is in an approved group.
o	Row filters: restrict by domain, or by business unit or state where needed.
o	Policies set at catalog or schema level apply to every new table that carries the tag, so no manual grant is needed.
•	Give access to groups (pfs-readers, pfs-engineers, pfs-admins), give the production service principal write access only, and keep work tables private.
•	Use Delta Sharing for consumers outside your workspace instead of copying data, and share only the gold and published tables.
Phase 4: Monitoring and continuous compliance
•	Run a scheduled audit job that flags any table or column without required tags, untagged sensitive columns, or tables without an owner or comment.
•	Use Data Quality Monitoring on the core tables (ftr_pfs_item_loc_day, ftr_pfus_item_loc_day, the channel-comp tables) for freshness, completeness and anomaly detection. It complements your check_step checks.
•	Review access with audit logs (who reads restricted columns), and use lineage to trace the impact of upstream biw changes.
•	Publish trusted gold tables under a Discover domain and mark them with certified/deprecated tags.
One-line summary: "A config-driven, multi-environment Delta lakehouse (about 110 feature tables, 118 TB, 1.6 TB written a day) with idempotent date-partitioned loads, shallow-cloned sources, API-driven orchestration and built-in quality gates. The next step is metadata-driven governance: automated classification, governed tags and tag-based policies managed from the same YAML configs."
