I measured your real PFUS load to answer this. In short: MERGE won't help, liquid clustering won't speed up the load, and serverless at list price would roughly double your daily compute cost. The gains are elsewhere.
How the PFUS load actually runs (from ftr_pfus_item_loc_day change history)
•	Each day partition is about 19.3M rows and about 1.2 GB, with 205 columns. That's a dense item × store grid: 44.3K items × 912 stores.
•	Every day the pipeline deletes the whole 113-day window (DAY_IDNT 2027096–2027208) in four 30-day chunks, plus the day rolling off the back. It then appends four chunks in parallel: about 2.2 bn rows and 135 GB a day.
•	The deletes are almost free. They line up exactly with the partitions, so Delta just drops files and copies 0 rows.
•	The real cost is computing and writing 2.2 bn rows.
Why MERGE won't help: I compared the 28 Oct and 29 Oct versions row by row
Comparison	Rows changed per day partition
All columns	100%
Excluding the 2 audit columns	98.8%
Also excluding 17 customer-segment columns (AFF_*, LFSTG_*, SPND_*)	44.8%
•	RECD_LOAD_DT and PROCESS_KEY are stamped fresh on every row, every run.
•	The segment columns are held at item × store level, but they're copied into every one of the 113 days, so one refresh changes about 2.2 bn rows.
•	Even the remaining "real" changes (latest sales day, deliveries, last received, store hours) touch 45% of rows, spread across every file.
•	So a MERGE would still rewrite every file in all 113 partitions. On top of that it would add a 2.2 bn × 2.2 bn key join. It would cost more than what you do today.
What actually helps the load
•	Single atomic replace instead of DELETE then APPEND. Today the delete committed at 21:04 and the appends finished at 21:10 UTC. For about 6 minutes, readers saw 2.2 bn rows missing. A single replace-by-date-range commit (Delta's REPLACE WHERE) closes that gap at the same cost.
•	Move the item × store attributes out of the daily table. Put the segment, latest-sales and delivery-snapshot columns in a small "current" table (about 19M rows) and join them in the view. Then a segment refresh is a 19M-row write, not 2.2 bn. This is the only change that shrinks the daily rewrite.
•	Audit columns: keep RECD_LOAD_DT and PROCESS_KEY, but don't count them as data changes.
•	Check the far-future days. The table holds 267 more days beyond the window (DAY_IDNT up to 2028111, 5.2 bn rows) that this daily job doesn't rewrite. Worth confirming they're still meant to be there.
Liquid clustering
•	Your day partitions are already about 1.2 GB, with healthy files around 150 MB, so they aren't too big.
•	Partitioning by DAY_IDNT is what keeps your deletes free.
•	Liquid clustering only helps readers who filter by item or store inside a day. For PFUS, which is fully rewritten daily, you'd re-cluster 135 GB every day: extra compute for no gain in the load. Keep the day partitions.
•	For PFS (ftr_pfs_item_loc_day: 9.3 TB, 11 years, only about 0.8 bn rows written a day), clustering by DAY_IDNT, LOC_IDNT / ITEM_IDNT is worth it. History is stable, so you cluster it once and every model-training read benefits.
Classic vs serverless: cost for one day
Your pipeline warehouse is a classic 2X-Large (144 DBU per cluster-hour), running 1–2 clusters with a 15-minute auto-stop. It uses about 500 DBU a day. On 28 Oct it used 515.6 DBU, which matches the sum of the hourly figures:
Time (UTC)	DBU	Activity
09:00	48	10 queries, 10 minutes of runtime
15:00	46	10 queries, 12 minutes of runtime
18:00–21:59 (main batch)	422	about 1,420 queries
	Classic (today)	Serverless, same DBUs	Serverless, idle removed
Price per DBU (list)	$0.22	$0.95 (Australia East)	$0.95
DBU cost per day	about $110	about $475	about $390
Azure VM cost per day	about $160 (estimate)	included	included
Total per day	about $270	about $475	about $390
•	Breakeven: serverless only matches classic if it uses about 43% fewer DBUs (about 285 a day).
•	Main batch: this is dense work, so cutting idle time won't get you there. Only a big runtime speed-up would, which you'd need to test with a pilot.
•	Sporadic runs: the two small daytime runs are the exception. They spend about 94 DBU a day (18%) on 22 minutes of queries, mostly idle and start-up time. Those are worth moving to a smaller serverless warehouse. Keep the main batch on classic unless a pilot shows a large speed-up.
