Story 1: Idempotency vs incremental loads
Likely question: "Tell me about a design trade-off you made."
•	Situation: PFUS rewrites a 113-day window every day: about 2.2 bn rows and 135 GB. Stakeholders asked whether we should switch to incremental MERGE to save compute.
•	Task: Choose between keeping the simple, idempotent reload and adding the complexity of incremental loads.
•	Action:
o	Compared two consecutive days and found 45–100% of rows changed. A MERGE would rewrite every file anyway, plus add a 2.2 bn-row join.
o	Set out what each pattern gives us:
o		Delete and reload by date (current)	MERGE
Rerun after failure	Safe, same result	Needs careful handling of partial state
Backfill	Just change run_from_day_idnt	Separate logic needed
Cost at our change rate	Baseline	Higher
Debugging	One date range = one unit	Row-level history to untangle
•	Result: We kept the idempotent pattern on purpose, because simplicity and safe reruns were worth more than theoretical savings.
•	Line to land: "Idempotency is a feature I chose to pay for. At a 45%+ change rate it's also the cheaper option."
Story 2: Backfill without disrupting production
Likely question: "How have you handled a large reprocessing or a logic change?"
•	Situation: A logic fix had to be applied to history in a table with about 19.3M rows per day. A full rebuild in one go would have overloaded the shared warehouse during the daily batch window.
•	Task: Reprocess history safely, without breaking the daily SLA.
•	Action: 
o	Used run_from_day_idnt with max_days_increment: 30 to split the range into 30-day chunks.
o	Capped concurrency with max_parallel_jobs so the backfill couldn't starve the daily run.
o	Because each chunk is idempotent, a failed chunk is simply rerun with no clean-up.
o	The count and primary-key checks validated every chunk before the next one started.
•	Result: History was corrected chunk by chunk. Failures were contained to 30 days, the daily load never missed, and the backfill needed no new code, only config changes.
•	Trade-off to name: "Smaller chunks mean more runs and longer total elapsed time, but less damage when one fails. I tuned chunk size to balance the two."
Story 3: Data quality gates vs speed
Likely question: "How do you balance data quality against delivery speed?"
•	Situation: Downstream forecasting models consume the item × store × day tables. A duplicate key or a missing day silently skews a forecast.
•	Task: Stop bad data reaching consumers without slowing every load.
•	Action: 
o	Made count_date and primary_keys_date mandatory checks in every feature config.
o	Explained the cost honestly: the duplicate check scans the loaded window, adding minutes per run.
o	Argued the alternative is worse: a bad forecast reaches store ordering, which costs far more than minutes of compute.
o	Scoped the checks to the loaded dates (_date, not _all), so they cost the same whether the table is 400 days or 11 years.
•	Result: Bad loads fail fast and are fixed by rerunning, and consumers trust the tables. Every table also gets quality checks automatically because they're part of the template.
•	Line to land: "Run checks per load window, not per table. That keeps validation cost flat as the table grows."
Story 4: The 6-minute data gap
Likely question: "Tell me about a risk you found and how you handled it."
•	Situation: The change history showed the delete committed at 21:04, but the four parallel appends only finished at 21:10. For about 6 minutes, readers saw 2.2 bn rows missing. The same idempotent pattern that makes reruns safe had a window where the table was incomplete.
•	Task: Assess and communicate the risk, and fix it without losing what makes the framework safe to rerun.
•	Action: 
o	Likelihood: low today, because consumers read later in the day. It rises as consumers move towards near-real-time reads.
o	Impact: high. A model trained or scored during the gap gets a silently incomplete dataset, and the checks would still pass afterwards.
o	Evidence: the commit timestamps from the table's change history, not a hypothesis.
o	Mitigation: replace the separate delete and append with a single atomic replace-by-date commit. It stays just as idempotent and costs the same, with no window where data is missing.
o	Short-term control: schedule consumers after the load finishes and the checks pass, using the existing dependency config.
•	Result: The risk went into the register with an owner and a fix planned in the framework itself, so all of about 110 tables benefit, not just one.
•	Line to land: "Idempotent isn't the same as atomic. I kept the first and added the second."
