Your framework is a SQL-first, config-driven batch lakehouse, and for dense, mostly-rewritten time-series that's the right base. Lakeflow Spark Declarative Pipelines (SDP, formerly called DLT), Auto Loader, PySpark and change data feed (CDF) each fit specific gaps. Use them there, not as a full replacement.
When to use Lakeflow Spark Declarative Pipelines
Use SDP when	Keep your framework when
The source changes a little each day and incremental refresh can process only the changes	Most rows change daily. PFUS changes 45–100% of rows, so incremental refresh would do a near-full recompute anyway
You want built-in lineage, retries, quality rules (expectations) and dependency ordering without writing them	You already have this through YAML: dependencies, check_step, idempotent date-window reloads
New feature tables or small dimensions (item, store, promo, cost reference), or the proposed item × store current table	The ~110 existing tables, where migrating means rewriting configs with little gain
Streaming or near-real-time freshness is needed	Daily batch with a fixed window
•	Trade-off to state: SDP runs on its own pipeline or serverless compute. For your dense 3-hour batch, the classic SQL warehouse is about $270 a day versus an estimated $390–475 on serverless at list price. SDP has to earn that back through incremental processing, which your PFUS data doesn't allow.
•	Best fit: a hybrid. Your framework handles the heavy item × store × day rebuilds, and SDP handles the low-change dimension and snapshot tables that feed them.
Auto Loader: why you didn't need it, and when you would
•	Your sources are Delta tables in the biw schema, which you bring in as shallow clones. Auto Loader is for files landing in cloud storage (CSV, JSON, Parquet drops), so it wasn't relevant.
•	You'd need it for:
o	vendor or supplier file feeds;
o	manual uploads such as range or promo spreadsheets;
o	any source that arrives as files rather than tables.
In those cases it gives you incremental, exactly-once ingestion and schema evolution into a raw layer.
SQL vs PySpark notebooks
Stay in SQL (most of your work)	Use PySpark
Joins, aggregations, window functions for features	Logic driven by your configs: looping over tables or dates, building SQL dynamically (what your R engine does today)
Date-window delete and reload, checks	Complex algorithms: forecasting prep, statistical features, item-similarity work
Anything that runs well on a SQL warehouse	Calling machine-learning models, the feature store client or external APIs
	Data quality frameworks beyond counts and duplicate checks: distribution or drift checks
•	Rule of thumb: SQL for transformations, Python for orchestration and logic. With serverless notebooks, R isn't supported, so new framework code should be in Python.
Jobs with many tasks
•	Use one Lakeflow Job with many tasks when tables depend on each other. Today the modelstore_dependencies.yaml graph is run through REST calls from R. As native tasks, you'd get: 
o	a dependency graph and UI;
o	retries for just the failed task, and repair-and-rerun from the failed task onward;
o	conditional tasks (for example, a fallback if upstream is late);
o	for-each tasks (for example, one per 30-day backfill chunk);
o	per-task compute, and cost attributed to each job.
•	Pattern: 
1.	A wait-for-upstream task.
2.	Parallel feature tasks (cost, promo, sales and so on).
3.	Item-location-day assembly.
4.	Channel and comparison tables.
5.	Quality checks.
6.	Publish or views.
•	Define it as code: generate the job definition from your YAML dependencies using Declarative Automation Bundles, so the config stays the single source of truth.
When change data capture (CDC) or change data feed fits here
•	Not on PFUS item-location-day. Every daily reload rewrites all rows, so CDF would emit about 2.2 bn deletes plus 2.2 bn inserts a day, with no downstream saving.
•	Good fits: 
o	Upstream sources that change a little each day (cost, price, promo, range, item and store attributes). Read only the changes and recompute only the affected item × store × day rows. This is where real compute savings come from.
o	The proposed item × store current table (segments, latest sales, deliveries). It's small (19M rows) and naturally change-driven, so it's ideal for CDF or SDP's built-in CDC handling.
o	PFS history (9.3 TB, but only about 0.8 bn rows written a day). Downstream copies such as gold or shared tables can consume only the changes instead of re-reading partitions.
o	Slowly changing dimensions (item hierarchy, store attributes), where you need history (SCD type 2).
•	Caveat: change data feed has to be enabled on the table you read changes from. A shallow clone starts its own history at clone time, so for source-level changes, read from the source table or confirm CDF is enabled on it.
Reference architecture for a scalable, reliable lakehouse like this
Layer	Pattern	Tools
Ingest	Tables: shallow clone or read source changes. Files: Auto Loader	Delta, change data feed, Auto Loader
Raw / staging	Append-only, schema evolution, audit columns	Delta, Unity Catalog
Dimensions and snapshots	Incremental, change-driven, SCD where needed	SDP materialized views and CDC handling
Heavy features (item × store × day)	Config-driven, idempotent atomic replace by date window, chunked backfill	Your framework on a SQL warehouse
Quality	Count and duplicate checks per load window, plus expectations and anomaly monitoring	check_step, SDP expectations, Data Quality Monitoring
Serve	Views, gold schema, Delta Sharing, clustering on stable history (PFS)	Views, liquid clustering, Delta Sharing
Orchestrate	Multi-task Lakeflow Jobs generated from the YAML dependencies	Jobs, Declarative Automation Bundles
Govern	Governed tags, automated classification, tag-based policies, lineage	Unity Catalog
Recover	Idempotent reruns, time travel, shallow-clone release snapshots	Delta
One-line interview answer: "I match the tool to how much the data changes. Low-change dimensions go incremental with SDP and change data feed. Dense, fully-changing time-series stay on a config-driven, idempotent SQL batch. Everything is orchestrated as multi-task jobs defined in code, and governed in Unity Catalog."

